12.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The corner position is three Black fragments

* Gc = C1–C2 : {B1,B2}
* Ga = A2    : {A1,A3,B2}
* Gb = B3–B4 : {A3,A4,B2}

Touching points are A1,A3,A4,B1,B2. White’s wall at B5,C3,C4,D1,D2 makes A-file net relevant.

1.B1
Gc is left 1-liberty on B2. 1…B2 is legal on a group basis. B2 joins Ga–Gb–Gc into M with distinct outside neighbours A1,A3,A4. The move is not self-atari for the merged group and does not capture White B1 in one move; White B1 still retains A1. White B1 is then 1-liberty on A1 and is taken by 2…A1. C1–C2 is saved on the new liberty B2. The per-stone suicide claim for …B2 after 1.B1 is a group-liberty error.

1.B2
White B2 is in atari on B1 and strips B2 from Ga,Gb,Gc. Immediate saving defence is 1…B1×B2. After 1.B2 1…B1

B1–C1–C2 : {A1,B2}
A2       : {A1,A3,B2}
B3–B4    : {A3,A4,B2}

A1 and B2 are suicide for White after the capture. The only legal touching continuations are A3,A4.

2.A3 is met by 2…A4×A3 or by the connecting 2…B2 which merges A2,B1,B3,C2 into M with distinct outside liberties A1,A4 and puts White A3 in atari on A4. 3.A4 is then met by 3…A5 or by the same 2…B2 connection which nets the White A3–A4 stones and restores A4 to Black, leaving M with A1,A4. The same symmetry holds for 2.A4.

Alternative first defences after 1.B2:
1…A1 leaves C1–C2 1-liberty on B1 and A1–A2 2-liberty on A3,B1; 2.B1 captures C1–C2.
1…A3 joins A2 to Gb with liberties A1,A4 and C1–C2 stays 1-liberty on B1; 2.B1 captures C1–C2.
1…A4 leaves Gb with A3,A5 and C1–C2 1-liberty on B1; 2.B1 captures C1–C2. In all cases the empty point B2 is re-opened after the capture 1…B1 and remains a second liberty for B1–C1–C2, and the connecting defence 2…B2 restores ≥2 distinct outside liberties to the merged left side.

1.A1 is 1…B1×A1, 1.A3 is 1…A4×A3, 1.A4 is met by 1…B2 which gives A2–B2–B3–B4–C1–C2 the liberties A1,A3,B1 and leaves White A4 with A5 only.

Hence every adjacent first White move is either immediately captured or met by the legal connecting defence …B2 after 1.B1 and the capturing defence …B1 after 1.B2, after which Black can keep a group with ≥2 distinct outside liberties. The persistent claim for {B2} as a forced killer ignores the 1…B1 capture of the sacrificial White B2 and the subsequent 2…B2 connecting/A5 recapture defence that breaks the A-file net. No first White move initiates a forced capture sequence against perfect Black defence; the consistent synthesis is the empty set {}.

